# Medora Enterprise Authorship Immutability (Master Constitution)

**Short form (every clinical feature):**

> **Authors can correct themselves. Everyone else documents forward.**

This is a **medical-record integrity** rule — not a UI rule. It applies to every clinical documentation engine in Medora, present and future.

## CORE INVARIANT

**User B MUST NEVER modify User A’s authored clinical documentation.**

Applies to (non-exhaustive): Nursing Admission · Nursing Assessment / reassessments · Nursing notes · Provider H&P · Provider progress notes · Consult notes · Procedure documentation · Technician/PCT · Respiratory · PT/OT/rehab · Pharmacy clinical documentation · Laboratory documentation · Radiology documentation · Care Plans and discipline contributions · Case management · Social work · Discharge planning · Patient education · any future clinical module.

## 1. Authorship is immutable

Every authored clinical document/event MUST retain:

- `authorUserId` (or equivalent durable owner field)
- author display name
- credentials / profession / role at authorship
- authored / documented timestamp
- facility
- encounter
- version / lineage

Once User A authors an entry, authorship MUST NEVER be reassigned to User B.

**Never** treat as document owner: patient assignment, shift assignment, current RN, current attending, department assignment, ADMIN status, or facility membership alone.

## 2. User A may correct User A

The original author may correct their own documentation through Medora’s **governed** correction / amendment workflow.

For signed / final documentation:

- preserve original version
- require correction reason
- append amendment / corrected version (do not silently overwrite)
- preserve original signer / time
- identify correcting user
- record before/after or equivalent lineage
- retain audit history

No second clinician signature is required merely for the original author to correct their own documentation, unless a separate regulated cosign workflow explicitly requires it.

## 3. User B documents a new entry

When User B assumes care, User B creates **new** documentation under User B’s identity.

- RN A assessment → later RN B **reassessment** (not edit of A)
- Provider A progress note → later Provider B progress note / addendum (not rewrite of A)

## 4. Draft ownership

Applies **before** signing.

If User A starts a clinical document with an authoritative owner:

- User A may continue editing it
- User B sees it read-only
- User B creates their own appropriate documentation

**Do not** transfer draft ownership because shifts changed.

Signed documents with **unresolvable** owner (`signed` but no resolvable `signedByUserId` / owner) → **READ ONLY / OWNER UNRESOLVED** — never claimable by inference.

## 5. Administrative exception is not editing

ADMIN / privileged governance users MUST NOT rewrite another clinician’s documentation.

If policy permits Entered in Error / Void / Superseded / Administrative Correction, that MUST be a **new governance event** preserving the original and recording actor, reason, timestamp, affected document.

Never silently alter User A’s content.

## 6. Shared server-side enforcement

Do **not** implement this only by disabling buttons.

Prefer one enterprise authorship authorization resolver (shared), conceptually:

```ts
canModifyClinicalDocumentation({
  currentUserId,
  authorUserId,
  documentState, // DRAFT | SIGNED | AMENDED | VOID | …
  action,        // EDIT | CORRECT | GOVERNANCE_VOID | …
})
```

Ordinary modification requires: `currentUserId === authorUserId`.

Any permitted governance action must use a **separate explicit action** and must never masquerade as author editing.

**Every write API must enforce this server-side.** Frontend controls are only a projection of server authority.

## 7. Concurrency

Even User A cannot silently overwrite User A’s stale copy.

Preserve optimistic concurrency / version checking → **409** on stale version.

No last-write-wins clinical documentation.

## 8. Medical record / Summary / Print

Summary and Print Entire Chart must preserve authorship.

Where appropriate show: author · credentials · date/time · signed status · amendment/correction indicator.

Summary must never blend multiple clinicians’ entries into one anonymous document.

## 9. Audit

Required for clinical writes / corrections: actor · author · encounter · facility · document/event ID · action · timestamp · version · correction reason where applicable.

Original historical clinical documentation remains recoverable.

## 10. Cross-facility

Enterprise Medora rule.

- No facility UUID branches
- No Haiti-only / Clinic-only / inpatient-only ownership models
- Same authorship invariant across all clinical settings

## 11. Before building feature-specific fixes

1. **Audit** existing clinical write endpoints for that domain.
2. Classify compliant / partial / noncompliant with evidence.
3. Remediate **only proven violations** — do not change all domains blindly.
4. Reuse the shared authorship resolver; do not invent parallel ownership engines per specialty.

## STOP — architectural review required

Do **not** ship without review:

- Role-only gates that allow editing another user’s clinical document
- ADMIN overwrite of clinical authorship content
- Assignment / shift as substitute for `authorUserId`
- Non-author edit of signed documents
- Implicit draft ownership transfer on handoff / shift change
- UI-only “read-only” without server rejection
